    </div>

    <!-- H1 Heading -->
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-white mb-6 leading-tight">
      HMAC-SHA256 vs Ed25519 Webhook Signatures: Cryptographic Speed, Asymmetric Verification & Replay Protection
    </h1>

    <!-- Quick Answer Box (Exact 45-60 words format with theme accent border) -->
    <div class="border-l-4 border-orange-500 bg-slate-900/80 border-t border-r border-b border-orange-500/30 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-10 shadow-2xl backdrop-blur-sm">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-orange-400 font-bold text-xs uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 font-mono">
        <span>⚡</span> Quick Answer: Symmetric Hash vs Asymmetric Elliptic Curves
      </div>
      <p class="text-slate-200 text-base leading-relaxed font-medium">
        <strong>HMAC-SHA256</strong> offers ultra-fast verification (~1.8µs) and low computational overhead, but relies on a shared secret: if compromised by any consumer, attackers can forge authentic webhooks. <strong>Ed25519</strong> provides asymmetric non-repudiation using public-key cryptography—consumers only store public keys, eliminating forgery risks entirely at the cost of higher verification latency (~45µs).
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Section 1 -->
    <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-12">
      <p class="text-lg text-slate-200">
        In distributed microservices and asynchronous SaaS integrations, webhooks represent reverse APIs where security guarantees must be established over untrusted public transit. When an event producer (such as Stripe, Shopify, GitHub, or an internal event bus) dispatches an HTTP POST request to an external consumer endpoint, the receiving server must definitively resolve three fundamental questions:
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>Authenticity & Integrity:</strong> Did this payload genuinely originate from the declared provider, and was it modified in transit?</li>
        <li><strong>Non-Repudiation:</strong> Can the provider mathematically prove or deny having authored the message?</li>
        <li><strong>Freshness & Uniqueness:</strong> Is this event contemporary, or has an eavesdropping adversary captured a legitimate historical payload and replayed it?</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        For over a decade, symmetric <strong>HMAC-SHA256</strong> has served as the default standard across the software industry. However, modern security architectures—spearheaded by the <strong>Standard Webhooks</strong> initiative and next-generation API protocols—are aggressively adopting <strong>Ed25519 (Edwards-curve Digital Signature Algorithm over Curve25519)</strong>. Understanding the trade-offs between symmetric speed and asymmetric cryptographic isolation is essential for enterprise platform engineers.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- H2: Cryptographic Primitives -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
      1. Cryptographic Primitives: Symmetric Shared Secrets vs Public Key Infrastructure
    </h2>
    <div class="text-slate-300 space-y-4 mb-8">
      <p>
        The architectural divergence between HMAC and Ed25519 stems from key symmetry:
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-6">
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
          <div class="text-orange-400 font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 font-mono">HMAC-SHA256 (RFC 2104)</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Hash-based Message Authentication Code uses a single shared secret key ($K$) combined with cryptographic hash function SHA-256 in an inner and outer pad structure:
          </p>
          <div class="bg-slate-950 p-2.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px] text-slate-400 my-2">
            HMAC(K, m) = H((K' ⊕ opad) || H((K' ⊕ ipad) || m))
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-400">
            Both sender and recipient hold the exact same secret $K$. Anyone who possesses $K$ can create valid signatures indistinguishable from the upstream producer.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
          <div class="text-emerald-400 font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 font-mono">Ed25519 (RFC 8032)</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            EdDSA utilizing the twisted Edwards curve $ -x^2 + y^2 = 1 - (121665/121666)x^2y^2 $ over finite field $\mathbb{F}_{2^{255}-19}$.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-slate-950 p-2.5 rounded font-mono text-[11px] text-slate-400 my-2">
            Sign: (R, S) = (rG, r + H(R || A || m) · a) mod l
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-400">
            Producer signs with private scalar $a$. Consumers verify signatures using public point $A = aG$. Consumers never touch the private key.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        <strong>The Key Leak Blast Radius:</strong> In a multi-tenant enterprise system with 10,000 customers receiving webhooks, if HMAC is used, the provider must either generate and store 10,000 distinct shared secrets or risk catastrophic cross-tenant forgery if a single global secret is used. If a consumer's database or `.env` configuration is compromised, an attacker can construct forged webhooks and inject false payment or order confirmations directly into the consumer's systems.
      </p>
      <p>
        With Ed25519, the producer maintains a single, tightly guarded private key (stored in an HSM or AWS KMS). The corresponding public key is published openly via a JWKS (JSON Web Key Set) endpoint or DNS TXT record. Even if every consumer's database is breached, not a single valid webhook signature can be forged.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Table 1: Cryptographic Benchmark Matrix -->
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Empirical Cryptographic Benchmark Matrix (2026 Server Hardware)</h3>
