            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">Zero Impact on Producer</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-300">Ed25519 prevents forged upstream events</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="hover:bg-slate-800/40">
            <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-white">Standard Adoption</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-300">Stripe, GitHub, Shopify</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-300">Svix Standard Webhooks, Discord</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-slate-300">Industry migrating toward Ed25519</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="p-3 text-[11px] text-slate-400 border-t border-slate-800 bg-slate-950/60 font-sans">
        *Benchmark measured on AWS c6i.2xlarge (Intel Xeon Platinum 8375C @ 2.9GHz) using Node.js v22 crypto engine with OpenSSL 3.2 backend.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- H2: Replay Attacks & Timestamp Drift -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
      2. Replay Protection: Timestamp Windows, Nonce Registries & Clock Skew
    </h2>
    <div class="text-slate-300 space-y-4 mb-8">
      <p>
        A mathematically authentic signature does not guarantee that a webhook should be executed. If a malicious adversary intercepts an authentic HTTPS POST payload containing an order checkout or credit top-up, they can resend the identical HTTP packet 10,000 times. Because the signature remains valid, an unprotected receiver would fulfill the order 10,000 times.
      </p>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white">The Three-Pillar Defense Architecture</h3>
      <div class="space-y-4 my-4">
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 p-4 rounded-xl">
          <div class="text-white font-bold text-sm mb-1 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="text-orange-400 font-mono">1.</span> Signed Timestamp Binding
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">
            The UNIX timestamp must be bound into the signature calculation. For example, in Stripe's protocol: <code>signed_payload = t + "." + raw_body</code>. The signature covers both the timestamp and the body. An attacker cannot tamper with the timestamp without invalidating the cryptographic signature.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 p-4 rounded-xl">
          <div class="text-white font-bold text-sm mb-1 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="text-orange-400 font-mono">2.</span> Strict Drift Tolerance Window
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">
            Upon receipt, verify that <code>Math.abs(Date.now() / 1000 - t) &lt; 300</code> (300 seconds / 5 minutes). Any webhook arriving with a timestamp older than 5 minutes or more than 5 minutes in the future (due to server clock skew) is rejected immediately with HTTP 400.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 p-4 rounded-xl">
          <div class="text-white font-bold text-sm mb-1 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="text-orange-400 font-mono">3.</span> Atomic Event Nonce De-duplication (Redis SETNX)
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300">
            Within the 5-minute allowable window, duplicate replayed requests would still be accepted unless tracked. Consumers must store the unique webhook ID (e.g. <code>msg_2bXyZ...</code>) in Redis using an atomic operation: <code>SET webhook:id:&#123;id&#125; 1 EX 360 NX</code>. If Redis returns <code>null</code>, the event has already been seen and is acknowledged with HTTP 200 without re-processing.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- H2: Timing Attacks -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
      3. Timing Side-Channel Vulnerabilities: Why String Equality Breaks Systems
    </h2>
    <div class="text-slate-300 space-y-4 mb-8">
      <p>
        Software engineers frequently make the critical mistake of verifying signatures using standard string comparison:
      </p>
      <div class="bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-800/50 rounded-xl p-4 font-mono text-xs text-rose-300 my-4">
        <code>// FATAL VULNERABILITY: Vulnerable to byte-by-byte timing attack<br/>
        if (computedSignature === request.headers['x-webhook-signature']) &#123; process(); &#125;</code>
      </div>
      <p>
        In modern CPU architectures, the <code>===</code> operator evaluates strings character by character from left to right, returning <code>false</code> immediately when the first mismatch occurs. If byte 0 matches, the execution takes slightly longer than if byte 0 failed. By dispatching thousands of carefully timed requests across high-precision network probes, an attacker can deduce each byte of the signature sequentially.
      </p>
      <p>
        To prevent timing leakage, signature validation must always utilize constant-time comparison routines such as Node.js <code>crypto.timingSafeEqual(bufA, bufB)</code> or Python's <code>hmac.compare_digest(sigA, sigB)</code>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- H2: Complete Implementation Code -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
      4. Production-Grade Implementations: TypeScript and Python
    </h2>
    <div class="space-y-6 mb-8">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white">TypeScript / Node.js: Asymmetric Ed25519 Webhook Verifier</h3>
      <p class="text-xs text-slate-400">
        Using native Node.js <code>crypto</code> (supports Edwards-curve Ed25519 since v12.0.0 without external dependencies):
      </p>
      <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-slate-800 bg-slate-950 p-4">
        <pre is:raw class="text-xs font-mono text-orange-300 overflow-x-auto"><code>import crypto from 'node:crypto';

interface VerifyOptions {
  rawBody: Buffer | string;
  signatureHeader: string;
  publicKeyBase64: string;
  toleranceSeconds?: number;
}

export function verifyEd25519Webhook({
  rawBody,
  signatureHeader,
  publicKeyBase64,
  toleranceSeconds = 300
}: VerifyOptions): { valid: boolean; reason?: string } {
  // 1. Parse header: format 't=1728100000,v1a=base64signature...'
  const parts = signatureHeader.split(',');
  const timestampPart = parts.find(p => p.startsWith('t='));
  const sigPart = parts.find(p => p.startsWith('v1a=') || p.startsWith('v1='));

  if (!timestampPart || !sigPart) {
    return { valid: false, reason: 'MALFORMED_SIGNATURE_HEADER' };
  }

  const timestamp = parseInt(timestampPart.slice(2), 10);
  const signatureB64 = sigPart.split('=')[1];

  // 2. Validate timestamp replay window
  const currentTimestamp = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
  if (Math.abs(currentTimestamp - timestamp) > toleranceSeconds) {
    return { valid: false, reason: 'TIMESTAMP_EXPIRED_OR_DRIFT_EXCEEDED' };
  }

  // 3. Construct canonical signed payload
  const bodyBuffer = Buffer.isBuffer(rawBody) ? rawBody : Buffer.from(rawBody, 'utf8');
  const signedPayload = Buffer.concat([
    Buffer.from(`${timestamp}.`, 'utf8'),
    bodyBuffer
  ]);

  // 4. Construct Public Key in DER SubjectPublicKeyInfo format for Ed25519
  const rawPublicKey = Buffer.from(publicKeyBase64, 'base64');
  // Ed25519 SPKI prefix: 302a300506032b6570032100
  const spkiPrefix = Buffer.from('302a300506032b6570032100', 'hex');
  const spkiKey = Buffer.concat([spkiPrefix, rawPublicKey]);

  try {
    const signature = Buffer.from(signatureB64, 'base64');
    const isValid = crypto.verify(
      null, // Ed25519 uses internal hash function (SHA-512)
      signedPayload,
      {
        key: spkiKey,
        format: 'der',
        type: 'spki'
      },
      signature
    );

    return isValid ? { valid: true } : { valid: false, reason: 'INVALID_SIGNATURE' };
  } catch (err: any) {
    return { valid: false, reason: `CRYPTO_VERIFICATION_ERROR: ${err.message}` };
  }
}
</code></pre>
      </div>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mt-8">Python 3.12: Hardened Constant-Time HMAC-SHA256 Verifier</h3>
      <p class="text-xs text-slate-400">
        Using standard library <code>hmac</code> and <code>hashlib</code> with timing attack prevention:
      </p>
      <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-slate-800 bg-slate-950 p-4">
        <pre is:raw class="text-xs font-mono text-emerald-300 overflow-x-auto"><code>import hmac
import hashlib
import time
from typing import Tuple

def verify_hmac_sha256_webhook(
    raw_body: bytes,
    signature_header: str,
    secret_key: str,
    tolerance_seconds: int = 300
) -> Tuple[bool, str]:
    """
    Validates HMAC-SHA256 webhook signatures with constant-time equality
    and replay protection matching Stripe and GitHub Enterprise specs.
    """
    if not signature_header:
        return False, "MISSING_HEADER"

    # Header parsing: 't=1728100000,v1=hexsignature'
    header_dict = {}
    for item in signature_header.split(','):
        if '=' in item:
            k, v = item.strip().split('=', 1)
            header_dict[k] = v

    timestamp_str = header_dict.get('t')
    claimed_signature = header_dict.get('v1')

    if not timestamp_str or not claimed_signature:
        return False, "INVALID_HEADER_SYNTAX"

    try:
        timestamp = int(timestamp_str)
    except ValueError:
        return False, "INVALID_TIMESTAMP_FORMAT"

    # Replay protection check
    now = int(time.time())
    if abs(now - timestamp) > tolerance_seconds:
        return False, f"REPLAY_EXPIRED: delta={abs(now - timestamp)}s > {tolerance_seconds}s"

    # Reconstruct signed message: "{timestamp}.{raw_body}"
    message_to_sign = f"{timestamp}.".encode('utf-8') + raw_body
    
    # Compute expected HMAC-SHA256
    expected_mac = hmac.new(
        key=secret_key.encode('utf-8'),
        msg=message_to_sign,
        digestmod=hashlib.sha256
    ).hexdigest()

    # Constant-time comparison to prevent timing side channels
    is_valid = hmac.compare_digest(expected_mac, claimed_signature)
    
    if not is_valid:
        return False, "CRYPTOGRAPHIC_SIGNATURE_MISMATCH"

    return True, "SUCCESS"
</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- H2: Interactive Sizer -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
      5. Interactive Webhook Verification Throughput & Latency Sizer
    </h2>
    <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-2xl mb-12">
